//? Title: Spectral layout: actual Laplacian modes and Jacobi work
//? Subtitle: Actual canonical histories, retained original fixtures and explicit controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A graph needs planar coordinates that keep strongly connected vertices nearby
without assigning semantic axes. Low-frequency Laplacian eigenvectors provide a
deterministic spectral construction up to sign and eigenspace ambiguity. We
inspect actual Jacobi pivots, retain the original four-vertex path, and expose
convergence and scan work.
)

\toc

> Mechanism and mathematical scope

For an undirected multigraph, L=D-A and the quadratic energy is

$$x^TLx=\sum_{\{i,j\}\in E}(x_i-x_j)^2.$$
\alttext(The Laplacian quadratic energy sums squared coordinate differences across edges, counting repeated edges.)

For a connected graph, constrain x perpendicular to the constant vector and unit
norm. The Rayleigh principle chooses the second-smallest eigenmode; the next
orthogonal mode supplies y. Multiple zero eigenvalues in disconnected graphs
invalidate a unique global Fiedler interpretation. This implementation preserves
the historical second/third sorted columns, so disconnected components may
select extra zero modes. Signs and bases within repeated eigenspaces are not
unique.

Jacobi starts with identity eigenvectors, chooses the largest off-diagonal
magnitude, and applies a symmetric rotation to reduce it. The table records
actual selected indices and post-rotation diagonals. It stops when the actual
maximum off-diagonal entry is below tolerance or reports the rotation cap as
unresolved. Positions are centered and both axes divided by the same maximum
radius, preserving their relative geometric scale.

A largest-pivot search scans the upper triangle in quadratic time. Up to order n
squared rotations therefore permits order n to the fourth scan work; the old
unconditional cubic claim did not account for those repeated full searches.
Dense matrices/eigenvectors use quadratic storage. This is not a sparse
eigensolver or a planarity guarantee.

> A tiny actual producing history

The table and native concept diagram read the same producing execution. Every
block below is a complete editable C++ program.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/spectral_layout_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>
#include <numeric>
#include <numbers>
#include <random>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <map>
#include <set>
#include <array>
#include <tuple>
#include <bit>
#include <datamunge/random/quality.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/spectral_layout.hpp>
int main() {
    namespace a = dm::algorithms;
    namespace g = dm::graphics;
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto r = a::spectral_layout_trace(2, {{0, 1}});
    std::print("| actual pivot i | j | largest before | rotation radians | actual diagonal after |\n| ---: " "| ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\n");
    for (const auto &s : r.history)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | `{}` |\n", s.first, s.second,
                   s.largest, s.angle, s.diagonal);
    g::Diagram concept_diagram(48, 1100);
    concept_diagram.node("n0", {0, 0}, "One undirected edge on two vertices");
    concept_diagram.node("n1", {5, 0},
                         std::format("{} actual Jacobi rotation", r.rotations));
    concept_diagram.node("n2", {10, 0},
                         std::format("Converged {}, eigenvalues {}",
                                     r.converged, r.eigenvalues));
    concept_diagram.edge("n0", "n1").edge("n1", "n2");
    concept_diagram.save_svg("build/spectral_layout_tiny.svg");
}
```
